<Base title="Editorial Policy" description="How the Heraldry Project uses original sources, translations, documented objects and visible uncertainty.">
  <PageIntro eyebrow="Sources before stories" title="How the project is edited." description="Heraldry belongs to many places, periods and languages. The evidence should remain visible wherever the teaching takes us." />
  <section class="section"><article class="wrap world-article">
    <h2>A growing research corpus</h2>
    <p>The project aims to build substantial, freely accessible teaching across heraldic traditions. An introductory lesson, a drawing tool or a regional chapter is a starting point. Coverage expands as sources can be checked and the teaching can be improved.</p>
    <h2>Read the originals</h2>
    <p>Archival catalogues, statutory texts, registers, seals, armorial manuscripts and historical scholarship should be traceable in their original language. Records retain their title, institution, locator, source link and consultation date. English explanations help readers approach those records; they do not replace them.</p>
    <h2>Keep different kinds of writing distinct</h2>
    <p>Original editorial synthesis, translated summaries, short attributed quotations and licensed or public-domain translations serve different purposes. The project labels them accordingly. Working translations and unresolved readings remain provisional until the stated review has actually occurred.</p>
    <h2>Preserve context and disagreement</h2>
    <p>Rules vary across jurisdictions and centuries. Evidence of historical use, a catalogue identification, a legal entitlement and a modern drawing are different claims. Teaching should show where an interpretation comes from, what remains uncertain and what evidence could change it.</p>
    <h2>Document the objects and respect their rights</h2>
    <p>Teaching diagrams are distinguished from authentic dated objects. Photographs and reproductions carry their own provenance and rights information, separate from the age of the arms they depict. Research exercises ask readers to inspect sources and defend conclusions that someone else can check.</p>
    <h2>Teach honestly and accessibly</h2>
    <p>The Academy's introductory foundation and research seminars confer no university credit or professional qualification. Software checks support publication but do not establish scholarly or specialist approval. Reading and practice should work on phones, with a keyboard and with assistive technologies, and corrections should make the evidence clearer.</p>
